<Base title="India" description="The regulatory and legal situation for tobacco harm reduction products in India.">
  <h1>Tobacco harm reduction in India</h1>
  <p>
    India has an estimated 100 million adult smokers and around 200
    million users of smokeless tobacco (SLT) — more smokeless users than
    smokers. Per the <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10263025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">second round of the Global Adult Tobacco Survey (GATS-2, 2016-17)</a>,
    the last full national round conducted, smoking prevalence sits at
    9.3-10.4% of adults and SLT prevalence at 21.38% — smoking-only 7.23%,
    SLT-only 17.94%, dual use 3.44%. SLT use is markedly higher among
    tribal communities specifically, at 32%. The most-used SLT products
    are khaini, gutkha, betel quid with tobacco, and zarda — not a single
    generic category.
  </p>
  <p>
    This section covers the current legal position on lower-risk
    alternatives, and the history of legal challenges to state and
    national bans.
